=====================

  S~S The Elevator Pitch:   an App co-existing with Events

------------------------------------------

    This SimpleSal App demonstrates the App and ssTEA co-existing while Events occur over Time.

------------------------------------------

    The processor in an embedded gadget powers up and runs software that exists in Time and Space.

    When you create a space where virtual objects exist and act over Time, you need a Time Keeper.

    When the software you write and put into a gadget exists over Time, events occur over Time.

    A SimpleSal App co-exists with ssTEA, software that is a Time Keeper and an Event Manager.

------------------------------------------

=====================

  S~S The purpose of the App:   app-events

------------------------------------------

    * The repo holds an example of the simplest, most basic SimpleSal App: the App and Events co-exist.

      The difference with the app-time basic SimpleSal App is that ssTEA Time and Data Events are used.
      This App also demonstrates the shared data space between the co-existing App and ssTEA software.

      The App uses the Arduino Built-In LED and the serial port to create a visual display of periodic
      moments in Time occuring.  Variations in the baud rate affect the visual of the serial port.

        * the App loop function executes an FSM that emits a single Ascii value each second.
          This FSM uses so-called classic or legacy Timekeeping methods.

        * the App loop function executes an FSM that turns ON the Built-In LED every 5 seconds.
          Each time the LED is turned ON, an ssTEA Time Event is scheduled to occur in 3 seconds.
          This FSM uses ssTEA Timekeeping methods.

        * when the Time Event occurs as configured by the App, the Event turns OFF the LED and ALSO
          resumes the Data Event.  The Data Event given Agency displays a sequence of Ascii values.

        * when the Data Event occurs as configured by the App and resumed by the Time Event,
          the characters "sent" by the Time Event are displayed (generating a longer Ascii pulse).

      A 1-second periodic serial port LED short pulse; a 5-second periodic serial port longer pulse;
      the Built-in LED is ON for seconds 1, 2, and 3; the built-in LED is OFF for seconds 4 and 5.

      Changing these numbers in the App to 100, 500, and 300 milliseconds changes the blink rate.

    * There are build-time configurations with this App, and with ssTEA.  These variations are
      concerned with the debug and App and ssTEA values displayed to the user via the serial port.

      ssTEA has run-time configurations controlling values displayed while the API and Events occur.

      The RELEASE metadata does not describe the build/run-time changes.

